<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0 -->
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	The one entitlement this app asks for, and it exists because of macOS.

	`KeychainSecretStore` sets `kSecUseDataProtectionKeychain` so that both
	platforms get the same item semantics and no login-keychain prompt. On iOS
	that keychain is the only one and an app is implicitly in the access group
	named by its own application identifier, so it worked with no entitlements
	file at all. On macOS the data protection keychain is opt-in, and opting in
	without naming an access group fails every write with errSecMissingEntitlement
	— "A required entitlement isn't present" — which the app reported as the
	secret not being stored while the connection carried on.

	The value is the implicit group: the team prefix and the bundle identifier,
	which is what iOS was already using. So this grants nothing new — it says
	out loud what was assumed — and needs no capability enabled on the app ID.
	It is the group CLAUDE.md names, $(TeamID).au.charlesmartin.currawong.

	Nothing else belongs here. In particular there is no App Sandbox — turning
	it on would also grant a keychain group, but it would bring the network,
	microphone and Bluetooth access this app is built on under separate
	entitlements — and no multicast entitlement, which PD-3 forbids.
	-->
	<key>keychain-access-groups</key>
	<array>
		<string>$(AppIdentifierPrefix)au.charlesmartin.currawong</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
